WEBVTT

NOTE
SYNTHETIC BENCHMARK FIXTURE. Fictional interview for the Verbatim eval set (Ledgerly study).
Participant p7: Hannah Cho, Senior Consultant, Fairlight Advisory (operations consulting), 65 employees. Not a real person.

00:00:04.265 --> 00:00:12.176
<v Interviewer>Hi Hannah, thanks for making time. Before we start, is there anything that would make this call easier for you?

00:00:13.041 --> 00:00:23.451
<v Hannah Cho>No, this is great. I'm on my laptop with my screen reader, so if you hear a little voice in the background, that's just it.

00:00:23.619 --> 00:00:25.305
<v Hannah Cho>I'll keep it quiet.

00:00:25.837 --> 00:00:29.998
<v Interviewer>Perfect. We'll record, if that's alright. It's about fifteen minutes.

00:00:30.583 --> 00:00:31.946
<v Hannah Cho>Sure, that's fine.

00:00:32.328 --> 00:00:35.482
<v Interviewer>Can you tell me about your role?

00:00:36.707 --> 00:00:44.638
<v Hannah Cho>I'm a senior consultant at Fairlight Advisory. We do operations consulting, mostly for regional hospitals and a couple of universities.

00:00:44.783 --> 00:00:51.702
<v Hannah Cho>The firm's about sixty-five people. I'm on the road, I'd say, three weeks out of four.

00:00:52.408 --> 00:00:55.919
<v Interviewer>So expenses are a big part of your life.

00:00:57.047 --> 00:01:00.281
<v Hannah Cho>[laughs] Unfortunately. Hotels, trains, flights, client dinners.

00:01:00.467 --> 00:01:03.733
<v Hannah Cho>I probably submit thirty, forty expenses a month.

00:01:04.367 --> 00:01:06.957
<v Interviewer>When did Ledgerly start for you?

00:01:07.628 --> 00:01:13.458
<v Hannah Cho>About a month ago. Our finance team moved everyone over in one go.

00:01:13.751 --> 00:01:22.995
<v Hannah Cho>Before that we had a different tool where you emailed receipts to an address and someone in finance typed them in.

00:01:24.126 --> 00:01:27.641
<v Interviewer>How did that old tool work for you?

00:01:28.145 --> 00:01:35.764
<v Hannah Cho>Honestly, really well, by accident. It was just email, and email I can do in my sleep.

00:01:35.992 --> 00:01:44.578
<v Hannah Cho>It was slow on finance's side, I think, which is why they switched. But for me it was the easiest thing in the world.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:47.955
<v Interviewer>How has the new one been?

00:01:48.338 --> 00:01:53.016
<v Hannah Cho>Um, it's been a mix. Some of it's just learning curve.

00:01:53.219 --> 00:01:57.816
<v Hannah Cho>But there's one thing that's been a real blocker for me, personally.

00:01:58.862 --> 00:02:00.197
<v Interviewer>Please, go ahead.

00:02:00.706 --> 00:02:04.767
<v Hannah Cho>So, I'm blind. I use VoiceOver on my phone for everything.

00:02:05.092 --> 00:02:10.528
<v Hannah Cho>And most apps, honestly, are fine. Most of the Ledgerly screens read okay.

00:02:10.622 --> 00:02:19.974
<v Hannah Cho>But the button to take a picture of a receipt — the screen reader just says "button." That's it. No label.

00:02:20.303 --> 00:02:31.001
<v Hannah Cho>And on the camera screen itself, the shutter doesn't say anything at all. I'm swiping around trying to find it and it just skips over it.

00:02:31.725 --> 00:02:34.109
<v Interviewer>How do you get around that?

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:43.912
<v Hannah Cho>The first week, I asked a colleague to take the photos for me at dinner, which — I mean, it's fine, people are nice.

00:02:44.074 --> 00:02:52.063
<v Hannah Cho>But it's not great to hand your phone to someone at a client dinner and say "can you do my receipt."

00:02:52.367 --> 00:03:01.794
<v Hannah Cho>Now I've found that if I take the picture with the regular camera first, and then attach it from the photo library, it sort of works.

00:03:02.044 --> 00:03:10.747
<v Hannah Cho>It's maybe six extra steps. But it means I'm doing receipts at night in the hotel instead of right there.

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:13.447
<v Interviewer>Did you report it?

00:03:14.067 --> 00:03:18.686
<v Hannah Cho>I did. I sent feedback through the app and emailed support.

00:03:18.853 --> 00:03:25.358
<v Hannah Cho>I got a nice reply saying they'd pass it on. That was three weeks ago.

00:03:25.675 --> 00:03:34.498
<v Hannah Cho>It's frustrating because it's literally one label. It's the one button the whole app is built around and it's the one I can't use.

00:03:35.007 --> 00:03:38.756
<v Interviewer>That's really important feedback. Thank you for sharing it.

00:03:39.973 --> 00:03:45.167
<v Hannah Cho>Of course. I'm used to it, honestly. But it'd be nice not to be.

00:03:45.999 --> 00:03:49.956
<v Interviewer>Has anyone at your firm said anything about it?

00:03:50.369 --> 00:03:58.503
<v Hannah Cho>My manager was great, actually. She offered to have finance accept my receipts by email again, as an exception.

00:03:58.776 --> 00:04:05.927
<v Hannah Cho>I said no for now, because I'd rather the thing just get fixed. But it's nice to have the option.

00:04:06.982 --> 00:04:11.991
<v Interviewer>Do you use other travel apps on the road? Airline, hotel apps?

00:04:12.946 --> 00:04:22.563
<v Hannah Cho>All of them. And they vary a lot. One airline app is wonderful, another one I've basically given up on and I just call.

00:04:22.687 --> 00:04:30.455
<v Hannah Cho>I don't expect everything to be perfect. I just notice when the one thing I need is the thing that's broken.

00:04:31.652 --> 00:04:35.059
<v Interviewer>Is there anything else that's gotten in the way?

00:04:36.086 --> 00:04:41.890
<v Hannah Cho>The other thing is less about me and more about travel. I'm on trains a lot.

00:04:42.008 --> 00:04:47.793
<v Hannah Cho>The corridor trains, you know, you lose signal every few minutes, tunnels, whatever.

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:55.909
<v Hannah Cho>I'll attach a receipt and it'll start sending and then the signal drops and — it doesn't seem to come back.

00:04:56.024 --> 00:05:01.530
<v Hannah Cho>Later I check and the expense is there but the receipt isn't on it.

00:05:02.275 --> 00:05:04.664
<v Interviewer>How did you find out?

00:05:05.749 --> 00:05:13.869
<v Hannah Cho>Finance flagged them. I had four expenses kicked back last month because of "missing receipt," and I know I'd attached them.

00:05:14.037 --> 00:05:18.562
<v Hannah Cho>Because with my whole workaround, I'm very deliberate about it.

00:05:18.883 --> 00:05:30.420
<v Hannah Cho>Same on planes. I'll do my expenses on a flight, because what else am I going to do, and the wifi on planes is, you know, a suggestion.

00:05:30.669 --> 00:05:33.026
<v Hannah Cho>Things just quietly don't go through.

00:05:33.972 --> 00:05:35.895
<v Interviewer>How does that feel?

00:05:36.906 --> 00:05:45.354
<v Hannah Cho>Exhausting, honestly. Because I've already done the six-step dance to get the photo in, and then it just evaporates.

00:05:45.993 --> 00:05:50.779
<v Interviewer>Let me shift a bit. How do your colleagues feel about Ledgerly?

00:05:51.864 --> 00:05:57.699
<v Hannah Cho>It's mixed, I think. Some people like the cards. The partners like the dashboards, I hear.

00:05:57.861 --> 00:06:07.873
<v Hannah Cho>I'm, um, I'm a bit of an outlier on how I use it, so I'm not sure I'm the best person to ask about everyone else.

00:06:08.267 --> 00:06:12.623
<v Interviewer>That's fair. Tangent: what's your favorite part of traveling for work?

00:06:13.068 --> 00:06:16.838
<v Hannah Cho>Oh, hotel breakfasts. I'm a sucker for a waffle machine.

00:06:16.918 --> 00:06:23.210
<v Hannah Cho>And honestly, the trains. I like the trains. I get a lot of reading done.

00:06:23.501 --> 00:06:24.850
<v Hannah Cho>Audiobooks, I mean.

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:28.322
<v Interviewer>What are you listening to right now?

00:06:29.218 --> 00:06:38.579
<v Hannah Cho>A big history of the Erie Canal. It's eleven hundred pages in print, which is why I'm glad it's audio. [laughs]

00:06:39.327 --> 00:06:41.268
<v Interviewer>Any travel disasters recently?

00:06:41.906 --> 00:06:49.808
<v Hannah Cho>A train broke down for two hours outside a station last week, and the café car ran out of everything except pretzels.

00:06:49.948 --> 00:06:52.920
<v Hannah Cho>I now have strong opinions about pretzels.

00:06:54.134 --> 00:06:58.500
<v Interviewer>What do you do while the train's stuck like that?

00:06:59.755 --> 00:07:06.953
<v Hannah Cho>Emails, mostly. And I tried to do expenses, which, as we discussed, did not go well. [laughs]

00:07:07.526 --> 00:07:12.210
<v Interviewer>Ha. Do you have a routine for doing expenses on the road?

00:07:12.679 --> 00:07:23.696
<v Hannah Cho>I try to do them every evening, in the hotel, with a cup of tea. Otherwise they pile up and I lose track of which dinner was which client.

00:07:24.042 --> 00:07:30.805
<v Hannah Cho>Before Ledgerly I did them on Fridays. Now it's nightly, partly because of the workaround.

00:07:31.317 --> 00:07:33.066
<v Interviewer>Does the routine work?

00:07:33.657 --> 00:07:41.393
<v Hannah Cho>Mostly. It's a lot more evenings on my phone than I'd like. My partner jokes that I'm dating the expense app.

00:07:41.731 --> 00:07:45.343
<v Hannah Cho>[laughs] I'm not. It's a very one-sided relationship.

00:07:46.005 --> 00:07:51.863
<v Interviewer>Once a receipt is attached, how's the rest of the flow? Choosing a category, a client?

00:07:52.438 --> 00:08:00.442
<v Hannah Cho>That part's okay. The fields are labeled, so VoiceOver reads them out, and I can pick the client from a list.

00:08:00.762 --> 00:08:06.763
<v Hannah Cho>It's not fast, but it's not broken. Which, honestly, is my bar for most software.

00:08:07.259 --> 00:08:11.537
<v Interviewer>What kind of client work are you on at the moment?

00:08:12.738 --> 00:08:21.103
<v Hannah Cho>A scheduling redesign for a hospital's outpatient clinics. Lots of interviews with nurses, which I love. They're the most honest people on earth.

00:08:22.283 --> 00:08:25.721
<v Interviewer>How does your firm think about accessibility generally?

00:08:26.301 --> 00:08:33.034
<v Hannah Cho>Better than most, honestly. We have a small group that reviews our client deliverables for it.

00:08:33.207 --> 00:08:43.333
<v Hannah Cho>Which is a little ironic, now that I think about it. We check our slide decks more carefully than some vendors check their apps.

00:08:43.569 --> 00:08:49.493
<v Hannah Cho>It's in a smaller city, so it's a two-train trip each way, every week.

00:08:50.542 --> 00:08:55.266
<v Interviewer>Okay, back to Ledgerly. If you could change one thing tomorrow?

00:08:55.921 --> 00:08:58.461
<v Hannah Cho>Label the button. Label the shutter.

00:08:58.653 --> 00:09:03.791
<v Hannah Cho>Let the screen reader find it. That's it, that's the whole wish.

00:09:03.973 --> 00:09:12.513
<v Hannah Cho>And if a receipt doesn't send, keep trying, or at least tell me. Don't make me find out from finance.

00:09:13.484 --> 00:09:17.351
<v Interviewer>Is there anything you'd want the product team to understand?

00:09:18.097 --> 00:09:22.566
<v Hannah Cho>That accessibility isn't a nice-to-have for the people who need it.

00:09:22.704 --> 00:09:34.104
<v Hannah Cho>If I can't do the main thing, I can't use the product the way it's meant to be used. I have to use it some other, worse way.

00:09:35.239 --> 00:09:37.658
<v Interviewer>Thank you, Hannah. That's really clear.

00:09:38.857 --> 00:09:41.465
<v Hannah Cho>Thanks for asking. Not everyone does.
